@@s969 Vietati salvo richiesta esplicita: <code>var</code>, assegnamenti, cicli (<code>while</code>, <code>for … do</code>), <code>return</code>, eccezioni, <code>println</code> e altri effetti, <code>Any</code>/<code>Object</code>.
@@s970 Scala di stile: variabili &lt; <code>val</code> · eccezioni &lt; <code>Option</code>/<code>Either</code> · ricorsione in coda &lt; ricorsione semplice &lt; fold &lt; funzioni dedicate · <code>if</code> &lt; pattern matching.
@@s971 Dalla settimana 2: ricorsione esplicita solo per iterazioni non standard; altrimenti HOF; <code>fold</code> solo se le altre HOF non bastano.
@@s972 Eccezioni richieste dal testo (<code>tail</code>, <code>drop</code>, <code>init</code>): lanciarle, non restituire <code>Nil</code>.
@@s973 Dettagli e punteggi: <a href="regole.html">Regole e divieti</a>. Messaggi del compilatore: <a href="errori.html">Errori del compilatore</a>.
@@s974 Frasi pronte in inglese
@@s975 Domande aperte del corso
@@s976 <i>toList fails on infinite lists because it is strict: it keeps forcing the tail until it reaches Empty, and an infinite list never reaches it.</i>
@@s977 <i>naturals.take(1000000000).drop(41).take(10).toList terminates because take and drop are lazy: only the elements that toList finally demands are computed, here about 51.</i>
@@s978 <i>naturals.forAll(_ &gt;= 0) never terminates because the predicate is always true, so forAll never finds a counterexample to stop at. exists stops at the first witness.</i>
@@s979 <i>foldRight on List is not stack-safe: after the recursive call there is still f to apply, so every element keeps a stack frame. foldLeft is tail recursive.</i>
@@s980 <i>The second argument is by-name, so it is evaluated only when needed. Otherwise many would call itself forever before reading a single character.</i>
@@s981 <i>listOfN is an extension method because Gen is an opaque type, not a class: there is no body in which to define a member.</i>
@@s982 <i>The laws are written only with the abstract operations of the trait, so they hold for every implementation.</i>
@@s983 <i>A pure function always returns the same output for the same input and has no side effects, so a call can be replaced by its value (referential transparency).</i>
@@s984 <i>Associativity lets us group the combinations in any way, so a fold with a monoid can be split and combined in parallel.</i>
@@s985 <i>I use Option instead of an exception because the possible failure is visible in the type, and the caller is forced to handle it.</i>
